      "page_title": "Horizontal Scaling and Latency Fixes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q43. How do you scale an API horizontally and why must it be stateless?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Run many identical instances behind a ==load balancer==. Stateless means any instance can serve any request.",
            "**Vertical** scaling hits a hardware ceiling; horizontal adds nodes.",
            "Keep sessions, carts and uploads in Redis, the DB or a token, not RAM.",
            "Stateful nodes force sticky sessions and lose data on restart.",
            "Autoscale on CPU or request rate; health checks drop bad nodes."
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "# stateful: breaks when scaled",
            "cart = SESSIONS[sid]  # local RAM",
            "# stateless: any node can serve",
            "cart = redis.get(f'cart:{sid}')"
          ]
        },
