      "page_title": "Async APIs, Timeouts and Circuit Breakers",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q45. What is the difference between synchronous and asynchronous APIs (202 Accepted and polling)?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "A ==sync== API does the work, then replies. An ==async== API replies **202 Accepted** at once and finishes later.",
            "Use async for work over a few seconds: reports, video, bulk imports.",
            "The 202 carries a `Location` job URL; the client polls it.",
            "Alternatives to polling: a webhook or push when the job is done."
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "POST /reports",
            "<- 202 Accepted",
            "   Location: /jobs/42",
            "GET /jobs/42 -> {\"status\":\"done\"}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q46. How do timeouts, retries with backoff and circuit breakers keep an API resilient?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "They stop one slow or failing dependency from tying up threads and taking the whole API down.",
            "**Timeout** bounds every outbound call; no timeout means waiting forever.",
            "**Retry** transient errors with exponential backoff plus jitter (see Retry Backoff).",
            "**Circuit breaker** fails fast once errors pass a threshold, then probes recovery.",
            "Retry only idempotent calls, with a retry cap."
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "for i in range(3):",
            "  try: return call(timeout=2)",
            "  except Timeout:",
            "    sleep(2**i + random())",
            "raise Unavailable  # breaker opens"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "hero": "autoscaling",
      "subtitle": "Scale, speed, resilience"
    },
