            ]
          ],
          "frame": "dashed"
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q48. How would you design a rate-limited public API for third-party developers?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Identify each caller by ==API key==, enforce a quota per key at the gateway, and return **429** with headers that tell the client when to retry.",
            "Use a ==token bucket== per key: allows bursts, holds a steady average",
            "Give plan tiers: free 60/min, pro 600/min, with per-endpoint costs",
            "Always send `Retry-After` and `X-RateLimit-*` headers",
            "Keep counters in Redis so all nodes share one limit"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests",
            "Retry-After: 30",
            "X-RateLimit-Limit: 100",
            "X-RateLimit-Remaining: 0"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "Algorithms in depth",
          "text": "Token bucket and Redis counters are on page 8; keys vs OAuth on page 5."
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Q49. How would you design the APIs for a checkout and payment flow?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Split it into ==resources==: cart, order, payment. Make the payment POST ==idempotent==, and let the provider's ==webhook== confirm the final status.",
            "Create the order first in state `pending`, then create the payment against it",
            "Send an `Idempotency-Key` so a retry never double-charges",
            "Never trust the browser redirect; trust the signed webhook",
            "Move the order through a state machine: pending, paid, shipped"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "POST /orders",
            "POST /orders/42/payments",
            "Idempotency-Key: 7c1e-9a",
            "202 {\"status\": \"processing\"}"
          ]
        },
